                        "entry_id": "4be8ab46-ff8f-377f-84ab-96a92afcd7f9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Lack of suavity; unpleasantness."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "obsolete",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1a94925a-314a-4c18-b482-88161ff8fa5c",
                                "sense_id": "ea1909e3-6ae1-30c9-8dc5-d23195340c0b",
                                "text": "All fears, griefs, suspicions, discontents, įmbonities, insuavities, are swallowed up and drowned in this Euripus, this Irish sea, this ocean of misery"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9ba108f6-6a3f-4b4a-af62-7ebb7eff31a3",
                                "sense_id": "ea1909e3-6ae1-30c9-8dc5-d23195340c0b",
                                "text": "It was not till this moment that Clym recollected her to be the person who had behaved so barbarously to Eustacia. It partly explained the insuavity with which the woman greeted him."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9ace1139-5f3c-47d0-9f71-4ead9b729917",
                                "sense_id": "ea1909e3-6ae1-30c9-8dc5-d23195340c0b",
                                "text": "He was bearded, blunt, thickset, and he wore gold-rimmed spectacles. In his bourrue-bienfaisance, in his honesty, and in his brusque insuavity towards such as would compel his sympathy without justification, he was the very type of the dogmatic family doctor—of the gruffly practical allopathist of the old school."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
